{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fdr915D5mEhFRNS3XP1U5Z","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n7\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n8\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n9\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n10\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n11\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n12\t\n13\t\\title{The Formulation Design Space for Neural Code Verification:\\\\A Controlled Empirical Study}\n14\t\n15\t\\author{Anonymous}\n16\t\\date{}\n17\t\n18\t\\begin{document}\n19\t\\maketitle\n20\t\n21\t\\begin{abstract}\n22\tExecution-free verification of code patches can be formulated as per-test binary classification (CLS), expected output generation (CWM), or trajectory-level scoring (TRAJ), yet no controlled comparison of these formulations exists. Practitioners lack guidance on which formulation to use under varying complexity. We present the first systematic comparison of three verification formulations across two model families (Qwen3-8B, DeepSeek-R1-Qwen3-8B), two complexity levels (function-level and repository-level), and multiple random seeds, trained on a new dataset of 87,000 per-test execution labels derived from SWE-bench Verified. Our findings reveal that formulations achieve statistically equivalent accuracy at function-level (Qwen3: 88.4/87.2/86.3\\%; DeepSeek: 86.9/86.8/84.2\\% for CLS/CWM/TRAJ), but diverge sharply at repository-level in a model-contingent manner. CWM collapse at repo-level is tokenizer-specific rather than formulation-inherent: unparseable rates drop from 54\\% on Qwen3 to 28.5\\% on DeepSeek. At function-level, CLS and CWM exhibit complementary error patterns, achieving 90.63\\% oracle ensemble accuracy (+2.2pp over either alone). Per-test CLS matches trajectory-level accuracy while providing diagnostic granularity at 6--9$\\times$ lower inference cost. These results provide actionable guidance for deploying verification in resource-constrained settings.\n23\t\\end{abstract}\n24\t\n25\t\\section{Introduction}\n26\t\n27\tAutomated code repair systems increasingly rely on execution-free verifiers to rank candidate patches when test execution is prohibitively expensive or unavailable at inference time. Given a code patch and associated test context, a verifier must predict whether the patch will pass or fail each test. Three formulations have emerged for this task: binary classification of pass/fail status (CLS), generation of expected execution outputs for comparison (CWM, after Code World Models), and trajectory-level binary scoring of the entire patch (TRAJ). Despite their widespread adoption, no prior work provides a controlled comparison across these formulations holding model architecture, training data, and compute budget constant.\n28\t\n29\tThis gap has practical consequences. CWM-style generation promises richer diagnostic information but requires the model to produce structured outputs that match exact expected formats. Classification avoids format compliance issues but discards the potential for fine-grained failure explanations. Trajectory-level scoring is efficient but loses per-test diagnostic granularity. Without controlled comparisons, practitioners cannot know whether observed performance differences reflect genuine formulation advantages or confounds from model scale, training data, or tokenizer properties.\n30\t\n31\tWe address this gap with a systematic empirical study. Our contributions are:\n32\t\\begin{enumerate}\n33\t\\item A new dataset of 87,000 per-test execution labels extracted from 423 SWE-bench Verified instances via Docker re-evaluation, enabling controlled comparison across formulations.\n34\t\\item Evidence that formulation choice is irrelevant at function-level complexity: CLS, CWM, and TRAJ achieve statistically equivalent accuracy across two model families.\n35\t\\item A characterization of complexity-dependent divergence at repository-level, where CWM collapse is driven by tokenizer-specific format compliance failures rather than inherent formulation limitations.\n36\t\\item Quantification of error complementarity between CLS and CWM at function-level (oracle ensemble: 90.63\\%), demonstrating that formulations capture complementary signals.\n37\t\\item A practical efficiency analysis showing per-test CLS provides 6--9$\\times$ inference speedup and 8$\\times$ batch capacity over trajectory-level approaches on commodity hardware.\n38\t\\end{enumerate}\n39\t\n40\t\\section{Related Work}\n41\t\n42\t\\paragraph{Execution-Free Code Verification.}\n43\tExecution-free verifiers predict test outcomes without running code. LEVER \\citep{ni2023lever} trains classifiers to rank code generations by execution likelihood. CodeT \\citep{chen2023codet} uses dual execution agreement for candidate selection. Code World Models \\citep{ding2024codeworldmodels} generate expected outputs of code execution as a proxy for actual execution. SWE-RewardModel approaches \\citep{zhang2025swebench} score entire trajectories with binary pass/fail predictions.\n44\t\n45\t\\paragraph{Process vs.\\ Outcome Supervision.}\n46\tOur CLS-vs-TRAJ comparison parallels the distinction between process and outcome supervision in mathematical reasoning. \\citet{lightman2023lets} show process reward models outperform outcome models for math verification. \\citet{uesato2022solving} demonstrate complementary benefits of process and outcome supervision. Our per-test CLS formulation provides process-level diagnostic granularity analogous to step-level supervision.\n47\t\n48\t\\paragraph{Verification in Code Repair Pipelines.}\n49\tCodeRL \\citep{le2022coderl} and AlphaCode \\citep{li2022competition} integrate verifiers into generation pipelines. Reflexion \\citep{shinn2023reflexion} uses execution feedback for iterative repair. Predictive mutation testing \\citep{zhang2023predictive} applies per-test binary classification to predict test outcomes without execution, establishing precedent for the CLS formulation.\n50\t\n51\t\\section{Method}\n52\t\n53\t\\subsection{Task Definition}\n54\t\n55\tGiven a code patch $p$, a test suite $T = \\{t_1, \\ldots, t_k\\}$, and associated context (source code, test bodies), the verification task is to predict whether $p$ passes each $t_i$. We study three formulations:\n56\t\n57\t\\paragraph{CLS (Per-test Classification).} For each $(p, t_i)$ pair, the model outputs a binary label $\\hat{y}_i \\in \\{\\texttt{pass}, \\texttt{fail}\\}$. Input is the concatenation of patch diff, test body, and relevant source context.\n58\t\n59\t\\paragraph{CWM (Code World Model).} For each $(p, t_i)$, the model generates the expected execution output $\\hat{o}_i$. The prediction is derived by comparing $\\hat{o}_i$ to the expected output: if $\\hat{o}_i$ matches, predict pass; otherwise fail. This requires structured generation of potentially complex outputs.\n60\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"16f629e4-e245-43ce-b614-1ac10bc00078","uuid":"172e991f-b96a-4943-bc77-36ec802f3417","timestamp":"2026-05-18T17:39:15.475Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260517T034206/worst/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{natbib}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\n\\title{The Formulation Design Space for Neural Code Verification:\\\\A Controlled Empirical Study}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nExecution-free verification of code patches can be formulated as per-test binary classification (CLS), expected output generation (CWM), or trajectory-level scoring (TRAJ), yet no controlled comparison of these formulations exists. Practitioners lack guidance on which formulation to use under varying complexity. We present the first systematic comparison of three verification formulations across two model families (Qwen3-8B, DeepSeek-R1-Qwen3-8B), two complexity levels (function-level and repository-level), and multiple random seeds, trained on a new dataset of 87,000 per-test execution labels derived from SWE-bench Verified. Our findings reveal that formulations achieve statistically equivalent accuracy at function-level (Qwen3: 88.4/87.2/86.3\\%; DeepSeek: 86.9/86.8/84.2\\% for CLS/CWM/TRAJ), but diverge sharply at repository-level in a model-contingent manner. CWM collapse at repo-level is tokenizer-specific rather than formulation-inherent: unparseable rates drop from 54\\% on Qwen3 to 28.5\\% on DeepSeek. At function-level, CLS and CWM exhibit complementary error patterns, achieving 90.63\\% oracle ensemble accuracy (+2.2pp over either alone). Per-test CLS matches trajectory-level accuracy while providing diagnostic granularity at 6--9$\\times$ lower inference cost. These results provide actionable guidance for deploying verification in resource-constrained settings.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\n\nAutomated code repair systems increasingly rely on execution-free verifiers to rank candidate patches when test execution is prohibitively expensive or unavailable at inference time. Given a code patch and associated test context, a verifier must predict whether the patch will pass or fail each test. Three formulations have emerged for this task: binary classification of pass/fail status (CLS), generation of expected execution outputs for comparison (CWM, after Code World Models), and trajectory-level binary scoring of the entire patch (TRAJ). Despite their widespread adoption, no prior work provides a controlled comparison across these formulations holding model architecture, training data, and compute budget constant.\n\nThis gap has practical consequences. CWM-style generation promises richer diagnostic information but requires the model to produce structured outputs that match exact expected formats. Classification avoids format compliance issues but discards the potential for fine-grained failure explanations. Trajectory-level scoring is efficient but loses per-test diagnostic granularity. Without controlled comparisons, practitioners cannot know whether observed performance differences reflect genuine formulation advantages or confounds from model scale, training data, or tokenizer properties.\n\nWe address this gap with a systematic empirical study. Our contributions are:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item A new dataset of 87,000 per-test execution labels extracted from 423 SWE-bench Verified instances via Docker re-evaluation, enabling controlled comparison across formulations.\n\\item Evidence that formulation choice is irrelevant at function-level complexity: CLS, CWM, and TRAJ achieve statistically equivalent accuracy across two model families.\n\\item A characterization of complexity-dependent divergence at repository-level, where CWM collapse is driven by tokenizer-specific format compliance failures rather than inherent formulation limitations.\n\\item Quantification of error complementarity between CLS and CWM at function-level (oracle ensemble: 90.63\\%), demonstrating that formulations capture complementary signals.\n\\item A practical efficiency analysis showing per-test CLS provides 6--9$\\times$ inference speedup and 8$\\times$ batch capacity over trajectory-level approaches on commodity hardware.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\n\\paragraph{Execution-Free Code Verification.}\nExecution-free verifiers predict test outcomes without running code. LEVER \\citep{ni2023lever} trains classifiers to rank code generations by execution likelihood. CodeT \\citep{chen2023codet} uses dual execution agreement for candidate selection. Code World Models \\citep{ding2024codeworldmodels} generate expected outputs of code execution as a proxy for actual execution. SWE-RewardModel approaches \\citep{zhang2025swebench} score entire trajectories with binary pass/fail predictions.\n\n\\paragraph{Process vs.\\ Outcome Supervision.}\nOur CLS-vs-TRAJ comparison parallels the distinction between process and outcome supervision in mathematical reasoning. \\citet{lightman2023lets} show process reward models outperform outcome models for math verification. \\citet{uesato2022solving} demonstrate complementary benefits of process and outcome supervision. Our per-test CLS formulation provides process-level diagnostic granularity analogous to step-level supervision.\n\n\\paragraph{Verification in Code Repair Pipelines.}\nCodeRL \\citep{le2022coderl} and AlphaCode \\citep{li2022competition} integrate verifiers into generation pipelines. Reflexion \\citep{shinn2023reflexion} uses execution feedback for iterative repair. Predictive mutation testing \\citep{zhang2023predictive} applies per-test binary classification to predict test outcomes without execution, establishing precedent for the CLS formulation.\n\n\\section{Method}\n\n\\subsection{Task Definition}\n\nGiven a code patch $p$, a test suite $T = \\{t_1, \\ldots, t_k\\}$, and associated context (source code, test bodies), the verification task is to predict whether $p$ passes each $t_i$. We study three formulations:\n\n\\paragraph{CLS (Per-test Classification).} For each $(p, t_i)$ pair, the model outputs a binary label $\\hat{y}_i \\in \\{\\texttt{pass}, \\texttt{fail}\\}$. Input is the concatenation of patch diff, test body, and relevant source context.\n\n\\paragraph{CWM (Code World Model).} For each $(p, t_i)$, the model generates the expected execution output $\\hat{o}_i$. The prediction is derived by comparing $\\hat{o}_i$ to the expected output: if $\\hat{o}_i$ matches, predict pass; otherwise fail. This requires structured generation of potentially complex outputs.\n","numLines":60,"startLine":1,"totalLines":258}}}
